WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: You've gone
through the migration process

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:05.370
and are ready to move your
first microservice to GKE.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.320
In today's episode,
let's see how.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.224
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.590
CARTER MORGAN: Let's consider
the Ice Cream Theory, which

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:21.660
is an online ice cream parlor.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.110
They're on this journey to
migrate their monolith website

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:26.320
to microservices, and
they're looking for guidance

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.515
in moving their first
microservice to GKE.

00:00:28.515 --> 00:00:30.640
So we'll walk them through
the migration in stages.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:31.710
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well, we'll need

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.020
to run an assessment of
their current environment

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:37.140
to determine how many public
endpoints we will need

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.730
and what needs to move.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:40.710
But we'll get to that
in the next episode.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.870
For now, let's assume
that they have picked up

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:45.480
a feature that makes sense
to start with this migration.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.150
CARTER MORGAN: Before we start
converting their chosen feature

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:51.630
to a microservice on GKE, let's
see how to prepare their GCP

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:52.140
environment.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.630
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Well,
before they migrate the legacy

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:57.090
components, they will
need a place to put them.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.780
And for that, they will
need a GCP organization.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:01.590
CARTER MORGAN: Then
they need to ensure

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:03.810
that only people with
the right authorization

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.580
can access the
resources, so they

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:08.550
need to design GCP
policies to control access

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:09.860
to the GCP resources.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:11.277
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
And once that's

00:01:11.277 --> 00:01:13.110
taken care of, it's
time to create a plan

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:16.590
to deploy the resources in
a standardized reproducible

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:18.750
way using
infrastructure as a code

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:21.840
utilizing tools like Deployment
Manager and Terraform.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.720
CARTER MORGAN: At this point, we
recommend the Ice Cream Theory

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.940
team to study GKE
and its features

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.080
so that they can tweak
them for their needs.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.360
This will mean changing
some of GKE's defaults

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.630
and maybe hardening
the cluster security.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.340
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Once GKE is set up,

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.830
it's time to build
the CI/CD pipeline.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.570
They can use their
existing tools

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:42.930
like Spinnaker or Jenkins, or
they can use the GCP products

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:45.270
like Cloud Build and
Container Registry

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:48.570
to build, store, and scan
those container images.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.180
Doing this early in the
project will avoid problems

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:52.495
when they are in production.

00:01:52.495 --> 00:01:54.340
CARTER MORGAN: And
remember the last episode

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:56.882
where we discussed communication
options between Google Cloud

00:01:56.882 --> 00:01:58.300
and on premise's data centers?

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:01.320
Well, they would need to decide
whether an API-based solution

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.480
like Apigee or a private
connection like Cloud

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:05.922
Interconnect is better
for their needs.

00:02:05.922 --> 00:02:08.380
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Now that we
have our GCP environment set

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:10.110
up, which is kind
of like preheat

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:12.430
the oven to start
the actual cooking,

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:15.900
it's time to start migrating
the legacy platform or with GCP.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.750
CARTER MORGAN: Overall, we
recommend that Ice Cream Theory

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.270
migrate there features
of the website one by one

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:24.570
to the new environment creating
microservices will require.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:27.390
Those microservices can call
back to the legacy system

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:28.290
whenever they need.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:29.873
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Migrating in stages

00:02:29.873 --> 00:02:32.010
like this has two advantages.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:35.280
Smaller projects are easier
to work with, minimize errors,

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.590
and costly mistakes,
and it lets you

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:40.802
handle each individual migration
without getting overwhelmed.

00:02:40.802 --> 00:02:42.510
CARTER MORGAN: The
first thing to analyze

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:44.640
is the dependencies of
the shopping cart feature

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:46.350
on the rest of the system.

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:48.450
We can do that by
reasoning through the steps

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.938
the user takes when
interacting with the website.

00:02:50.938 --> 00:02:52.980
The user makes the ice
cream selection and clicks

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.540
Add to My Shopping Cart.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.790
That triggers an API call
from the user's browser

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:58.470
to the shopping cart.

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:00.397
Once the shopping cart
receives the call,

00:03:00.397 --> 00:03:01.980
it checks whether
the chosen ice cream

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:06.010
is in stock by making an API
call to the inventory system.

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:09.490
If the item is in stock, the
shopping cart stores the info,

00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:12.520
something like Carter has one
instance of item strawberry ice

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:14.200
cream in the cart.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:15.820
Finally, when the
user checks out

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:17.600
and goes through
the payment process,

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:19.990
the shopping cart is queried
by the payment subsystem

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:21.420
to compute the total.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:22.930
And once the
payment is complete,

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:25.090
the payment subsystem
notifies the shopping cart

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:28.060
feature to empty the cart.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:30.650
So if we analyze
this at a high level,

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:33.250
this shopping cart feature
has four dependencies.

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:35.770
It is called by the front end
and the payment subsystem,

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:37.853
and it queries a database
in the inventory system.

00:03:37.853 --> 00:03:39.770
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Now
that we have identified

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:42.370
the dependencies, let's give
some thought to the choice

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:44.190
of database for this feature.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:46.960
A document database is
well-suited for storing

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:50.260
shopping carts since shopping
carts can be easily indexed

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:52.780
by user IDs, and you
don't need the power

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:54.890
of relational database here.

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:57.880
Cloud Firestore is a great
fit for this purpose.

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:01.100
It's a managed and serverless
no SQL document database,

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:03.730
so let's consider that for
our target architecture.

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:05.710
CARTER MORGAN: Now
comes the biggest piece,

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:08.310
migrating the actual
shopping cart feature.

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:10.480
Assuming Ice Cream Theory
can schedule a maintenance

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:11.272
for their website--

00:04:11.272 --> 00:04:12.910
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Wait, wait, wait.

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:15.550
I don't know when is
down time for ice creams.

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:18.229
I know I can eat ice creams
in the middle of the night.

00:04:18.229 --> 00:04:19.149
[CHUCKLING]

00:04:19.149 --> 00:04:20.140
CARTER MORGAN: I know.

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:23.020
Well, let's assume that they
can do scheduled maintenance.

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:26.170
First, they would need to
create a new microservice that

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:28.030
can call the inventory
system and uses

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:31.720
Cloud Firestore to implement
a shopping cart API.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.210
Then create a script that
extracts shopping cart entries

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:36.250
from the legacy
shopping cart system

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:38.513
and writes them the
Cloud Firestore.

00:04:38.513 --> 00:04:40.180
This script needs to
be written in a way

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:42.410
that it can be rerun
as many times as needed

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:44.020
but only copies
the shopping carts

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:46.587
that have been changed
since last time.

00:04:46.587 --> 00:04:48.670
Then create a script that
does the opposite, which

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:51.520
is it copies shopping
carts from Cloud Firestore

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:54.520
back to the legacy subsystem
in case they need to roll back

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:56.310
the migration.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:59.050
Then expose the shopping
cart API with Apigee.

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:01.300
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: They would
need to prepare and test

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:04.320
the modification to the
front end and payment systems

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:07.350
so they can call the new
shopping cart system.

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:10.170
Again comes the time to
run the data migration

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:13.840
script we prepared earlier
so all the data is in sync,

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.843
and we did not lose any shopping
cart records in the process.

00:05:16.843 --> 00:05:18.260
CARTER MORGAN: At
this point, they

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:20.610
need to put their website
in maintenance mode

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:23.610
and rerun the data
migration script.

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:25.110
Deploy the tests
and modifications

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:26.943
done to the front end
and the payment system

00:05:26.943 --> 00:05:28.560
on the legacy production system.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.020
Once done, they're ready
to disable the maintenance

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:32.342
mode in the website.

00:05:32.342 --> 00:05:34.800
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: And with
that, the shopping cart feature

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:37.080
of the Ice Cream
Theory website is now

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:39.420
microservice hosted
on Google Cloud.

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:41.250
The hardest step
of this process is

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:44.040
where they had to make changes
to the legacy payment and front

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:46.950
end system, but that gets
easier as they migrate

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:49.560
more and more features to
microservices because more

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:52.770
and more of the dependencies
will already be microservices.

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:54.630
As such, they are
more loosely coupled

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:57.810
than in monolithic application
and are easier to modify

00:05:57.810 --> 00:05:58.572
and deploy.

00:05:58.572 --> 00:06:00.030
CARTER MORGAN: For
more information

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:02.070
about alternative
migration approaches,

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:04.260
check out the article
written by Marco and Te'o.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:06.130
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well, there you have it.

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:08.700
If you are looking to migrate
a monolithic application

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:11.040
to microservices,
you've got a small taste

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.680
of the steps involved through
the Ice Cream Theory's use

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:14.370
case.

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:16.080
Stay tuned for the
next episode where

